To complete the RBAC module before advancing to GitOps (ArgoCD), we must look at how applications 
running inside the cluster actually authenticate against kube-apiserver using this identity.

When a pod needs to communicate with the Kubernetes API (for instance, an in-cluster CI/CD runner, 
Prometheus operator, or autoscaler), it doesn’t use user certificates or passwords. Instead:

Kubernetes automatically mounts a short-lived Projected ServiceAccount Token, a CA certificate,
 and the namespace identity into the pod at /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/.

The in-cluster client reads this JWT token and passes it in the Authorization: Bearer <TOKEN> header
 on every call to [https://kubernetes.default.svc](https://kubernetes.default.svc).

kube-apiserver validates the signature against the cluster's token signer and evaluates the pod's 
 permissions using the RBAC bindings we just created.



